latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Create a function `solve_cf133acc` that takes a `ColoredGrid` as input and returns a modified `ColoredGrid`.\n\n2. Create a deep copy of the input grid to avoid modifying the original.\n\n3. Get the dimensions of the grid (rows and columns).\n\n4. Iterate through each column of the grid:\n   a. Initialize an empty list to store non-black color positions and their colors for this column.\n\n5. For each column, scan from bottom to top:\n   a. If a non-black color is encountered:\n      - Add its position and color to the list.\n\n6. After scanning the column, process the stored color information:\n   a. Iterate through the list of non-black colors from bottom to top.\n   b. For each color:\n      - Start from its original position and move upwards.\n      - Fill all black (0) cells with this color until either:\n        * The top of the grid is reached, or\n        * Another non-black color is encountered.\n\n7. Repeat steps 5-6 for all columns.\n\n8. Return the modified grid.\n\nHere's a more detailed breakdown of the core algorithm (steps 5-6):\n\n```\nFor each column col:\n    Initialize empty list color_positions\n    \n    For row from bottom to top:\n        If grid[row][col] is not black (0):\n            Append (row, grid[row][col]) to color_positions\n    \n    Reverse color_positions list (to process from bottom to top)\n    \n    For each (row, color) in color_positions:\n        current_row = row - 1\n        While current_row >= 0 and grid[current_row][col] is black (0):\n            grid[current_row][col] = color\n            current_row -= 1\n```\n\nThis plan ensures that:\n- We process each column independently.\n- We preserve the original positions of non-black colors.\n- We extend colors upwards only through black cells.\n- We stop extending a color when we encounter another non-black color.\n- We handle multiple occurrences of colors in a column correctly.\n\nThis approach should work for all grid sizes within the specified range (1x1 to 30x30) and handle all possible color configurations within each column."
